cetrafs (0.9.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * No format version change: volumes made or grown by 0.9.2 mount as
    they are. One field is new in the inode record, carved from padding
    that is zero on every existing record. Several fixes below change
    how nodes arbitrate with each other and protect only nodes that run
    them: upgrade every node, and do not mount one volume from 0.9.2
    and 0.9.3 at once.

  * Fixed: a write could be sent to an address read off the disk
    without asking whether a file may live there. An extent naming the
    inode table made an ordinary write(2) overwrite inode records --
    on a shared LUN, for every node. Extent and tree-block addresses
    are now checked against the data regions before any I/O.

  * Fixed: punch and truncate on a file with more than 65535 extents
    edited only (n mod 65536) of them, losing the rest of the tree or
    leaving a punched hole holding data.

  * Fixed: preallocated space. fallocate(2) reaching a new extent leaf
    recorded that block as written, so reading it returned whatever a
    deleted file had left there; and writing into the middle of a
    preallocation in a full leaf dropped the rest of it, so a VM image
    could later hit ENOSPC on space it had reserved.

  * Fixed, crash ordering: several references could reach the device
    before what they name. Extent-tree edits waited on nothing while
    the allocation bits they rely on were only cached; a leaf split
    could be written destination-first, so a crash left blocks named
    twice; link and rename added the second name before raising the
    count; the journal replay consumed an entry without flushing its
    redo; a close published the record before its tree flush was known
    to have landed. Each now writes in the order whose crash leaves a
    leak, never a block shared by two files.

  * Fixed: write errors reported as success. Several synchronous
    record, bitmap and journal writes ignored their result, or judged
    it from a buffer flag that concurrent fresh reads clear -- either
    losing a failed write silently or failing one that had landed.
    Every synchronous buffer write now reports its own result.

  * Fixed, cluster: a node could free or reuse blocks a peer was using.
    The scrub could act on a verdict older than a released reservation;
    a node that took over a Zone Group kept a stale view of which Block
    Groups it owned; a released Block Group could be handed over with
    frees still cached; a freed metadata block's dirty buffer could be
    written over a peer's data; and an inode number could be reclaimed
    by a peer's scrub while being created. All are now arbitrated on
    the device.

  * Fixed, cluster: a create that drew an inode number at the moment a
    peer's scrub was reclaiming it could leave the number allocated
    under the deleted file's record, which still named blocks since
    given to other files -- fsck reported them shared, and freeing that
    record would have freed live data. The create now waits for the
    reclaim to finish and lets the bitmap, read fresh, decide who owns
    the number. Found by the three-node release run, which also gained
    a real write as its "node is serving again" probe.

  * Fixed, cluster: locks and metadata. A last close cleared the file
    lock before publishing the file's size and extents; an open could
    claim the lock on a reused inode number; a namespace-lock retry
    could clear a lock a local operation held; size, link count and
    times could be written back from a stale merge over newer values;
    two nodes moving directories into each other at once could detach
    both from the tree; and flock(LOCK_EX) waited forever on a holder
    that had died.

  * Fixed, cluster: defrag could relocate a file another node had open
    for read, freeing blocks that reader was still mapping through.
    Defrag now requires the file to be open nowhere else.

  * Changed, cluster: a write-open is refused while another node has
    the file open for read, as a read-open already was while another
    node held it for write -- one writer and nobody else, or readers
    and no writer. Both are checked inside the same atomic swap that
    takes the lock or publishes the reader. A node handing a file over
    with flock(LOCK_UN) is marked as a writer, so the destination can
    still take it and readers stay out until it is done.

  * Performance: a read-only open re-read the inode record from the
    device for every 4 KiB it mapped, holes included -- about 18 MB/s
    on a fast FC array, and a VM's qcow2 backing file or a base image
    shared by linked clones is read exactly this way. A reader now
    trusts its cached map between the file's change stamps, re-checked
    once a second: read-only reads went from 18 to over 2000 MB/s.
    Buffered reads also use readahead now, and an extent-tree block is
    checksum-verified whenever it is loaded from the device.

  * Changed: a node mounting after a crash watches its old registry
    row for two heartbeats (about 35 s by default) before re-attaching,
    and refuses the mount if another machine is using it. Machines
    with duplicate DMI UUIDs (cloned VMs, placeholder firmware) need
    the new cetrafs.node_uuid module parameter.

  * Tools: mkfs -f, fsck -r and growfs could decide a live node was gone
    -- a lagging clock, a slow heartbeat interval or a registry row
    caught mid-update -- and run on a mounted volume. The probe now
    waits out the peer's advertised interval, re-reads torn rows, and
    an offline repair's claim is judged by its renewals, never by
    clocks. fsck no longer repairs around journal entries a mount will
    replay, reports a queued free for a block still in use, and checks
    and repairs a directory's size. growfs refuses a superblock the
    mount would refuse.

  * Performance: a streaming file's speculative run grows up to 16 MiB
    (alloc_run_max_blocks), keeping sequential writes at full speed;
    fiemap, SEEK_HOLE/SEEK_DATA and hole punching no longer go
    quadratic on files with many extents; readdir no longer re-reads a
    directory from its first block on every call.

  * The module builds against the kernel 7.2 buffer-head API, and its
    version string carries a hash of its source, so nodes on different
    kernel builds can show that they run the same code. The test gate
    is 243 suites and 2257 assertions and has also run under KASAN;
    the image fuzzer now drives every path a mounted volume offers a
    hostile record, defrag, mmap and O_DIRECT included.

 -- Massimo Cetra <mcetra@gmail.com>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 15:51:21 +0000

cetrafs (0.9.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Most of this release comes from a detailed external review of the
    kernel module and the userspace tools by Matteo Croce (teknoraver),
    who read the whole tree and reported what follows -- including two
    faults nothing here could have found on its own: one reachable by
    any user with a writable file, and one that only shows on a kernel
    with pages larger than 4 KiB, which he runs and we do not. Our
    thanks: this is the most valuable thing anyone can do for a
    filesystem that is not yet 1.0.

  * ACTION REQUIRED for a volume still on on-disk format 0x0008 -- made
    by 0.8.x and never grown. The compatibility window that let the
    previous package mount one is closed, so this package will refuse
    it. Grow such a volume with 0.9.1 first, which stamps 0x0009, or
    reformat it. Volumes made or grown by 0.9 or 0.9.1 are unaffected
    and need nothing; the format is otherwise unchanged by this
    release.

  * Fixed: punching a hole in a range that covered no whole block but
    crossed a block boundary -- fallocate(FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE) of two
    bytes spanning the end of a page, for instance -- crashed the
    kernel. Any user with a writable file on the mount could reach it.
    The test that covers it now enumerates the boundary cases rather
    than sampling them.

  * Fixed: on a kernel whose page size is larger than the filesystem
    block size -- 16 KiB or 64 KiB pages, as several arm64
    distributions ship -- the online defragmenter invalidated only a
    fraction of the page cache it had to, because it passed block
    numbers where page indexes were wanted. Pages still mapped to the
    moved blocks survived those blocks being freed and reused, and a
    later writeback through one landed on another file's data. On a
    4 KiB-page kernel the two numbers are equal, which is why this had
    gone unseen; the module now states that assumption as a
    compile-time assertion and the mount reports what it is running
    on.

  * Fixed: a shared writable memory mapping was the one write path with
    no filesystem code at the store. Its blocks were allocated during
    writeback, with nobody to return an error to, so a store to a full
    volume was accepted and the data dropped -- only an explicit
    msync(2) said otherwise. Nothing excluded a page fault from a hole
    punch or a truncate either, so a fault in that window could leave
    blocks still carrying the mapping's bytes freed for another file to
    take. Allocation happens at fault time now, where out of space
    becomes SIGBUS as POSIX requires, and every mutation of a file's
    extent tree is serialised against the others.

  * Fixed: several ways a damaged or hostile volume could hang or
    mislead a node rather than being rejected. A directory entry
    claiming a name longer than its field read past the end of it; an
    extent recording a length of zero made fiemap (filefrag, backup
    tools) spin in the kernel forever, holding the file's lock and
    surviving SIGKILL; the Block Group table chain, the root inode's
    type, extent-tree depths and Block Group sizes were used without
    being checked against the device. All are validated where they are
    read now, and the image fuzzer was extended to reach them -- it
    could not produce a volume with a grown Block Group chain at all,
    so that code had never been fuzzed.

  * Fixed: fallocate(2) over a large range could not be interrupted --
    not even by SIGKILL -- and ignored RLIMIT_FSIZE, so a process
    limited to a small file could preallocate as much as the device
    could back.

  * Fixed, on a multipath SAN: a COMPARE AND WRITE that timed out was
    re-issued on another path, but a timeout means the host stopped
    waiting, not that the target did not execute the command. If the
    first had landed, the retry failed its compare, which the layer
    above read as an ordinary lost race and re-ran -- applying twice
    any update expressed as a delta rather than an absolute value, the
    free-space-accounting corruption this filesystem has paid for once
    already. An ambiguous outcome is now distinguished from a refusal
    and resolved by re-reading the record, which tells the three cases
    apart.

  * Fixed: a journal entry was consumed even when the write it
    protected had failed, destroying the only copy that could have
    redone it while the caller was told the write had landed; a failed
    replay did the same. The journal exists for that case and was
    discarding itself precisely when it was needed.

  * Fixed: a transient I/O error while revalidating a directory entry
    was reported to the VFS as "this name is invalid", which detaches
    any mount point on it. One unreadable directory block was therefore
    enough to silently tear down a bind mount, permanently, from a path
    that merely walked through it.

  * Fixed: evicting an inode freed its number before reading the extent
    tree, so a tree it could not read left the file's blocks allocated
    with nothing naming them, while the number was already reusable --
    a permanent, untraceable leak. The tree is fetched first now and a
    failure refuses the whole eviction, leaving an orphan fsck can
    report.

  * Fixed: recording a node's departure at unmount wrote the whole
    registry row back from a copy taken earlier, which could erase a
    fence a surviving peer had just recorded against it. That row is
    now changed by compare-and-swap like every other; no blind writer
    of it is left.

  * Fixed: three answers userspace reads were wrong. read(2) on a
    directory returned EINVAL instead of EISDIR; statfs left f_fsid
    zero, so every cetrafs mount looked like the same filesystem to
    anything that tells them apart by it, NFS export included; and
    fiemap never marked preallocated ranges unwritten, so backup tools
    reading extents copied blocks whose contents are not yet the
    file's.

  * Fixed: mkfs.cetra wrote the superblock before the structures it
    points at, so an mkfs interrupted partway left a volume that looked
    mountable over a stale journal from whatever cetrafs volume had
    been on the device before -- and mounting it replayed those entries
    into the new filesystem. The superblock is now the last write, made
    after everything else is flushed, and the flush is checked rather
    than assumed. Its size argument is also parsed strictly now, where
    a large value could overflow instead of being refused.

  * Fixed: a directory extent claiming an absurd number of blocks --
    ee_len is 31 bits read off the disk -- made fsck.cetra complain
    once per block rather than once per extent: hundreds of thousands
    of identical lines and tens of MB of output from a single crafted
    field, and a near-hang where the count was larger still. The
    content pass had been taught to judge a run before walking it; the
    namespace walk and two repair paths had their own loops and had
    not. Found by this release's own fuzz stage.

  * fsck.cetra repairs more and refuses more: it rebuilds an extent
    tree with an unreadable depth, validates what it finds in
    lost+found before trusting it, keeps a directory's names when the
    damage is confined to the tail of a dirent block, and declines to
    repair a tree it cannot walk rather than guessing. An offline
    repair now claims the volume for as long as it runs, so no node can
    mount underneath it; the claim is heartbeated and released.

  * The module builds against kernels through 7.2 and with clang
    (make mod LLVM=1), and can be built with KASAN (make mod KASAN=1)
    -- with the measured caveat, documented rather than assumed, that
    KASAN sees allocation bounds and not the field bounds most of the
    faults above lived in. The test gate is 192 suites and 1918
    assertions.

 -- Massimo Cetra <mcetra@gmail.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:30:00 +0000

cetrafs (0.9.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed: on a device whose atomic COMPARE AND WRITE unit is 4 KiB --
    a 4Kn target -- every publish of a file's own metadata failed, so
    fsync, fdatasync, writeback and closing a file all returned
    EINVAL. Under qemu it showed as the first flush after a write
    failing, while a second flush appeared to succeed because nothing
    was left to write. Devices with 512-byte logical blocks were never
    affected. No volume was damaged by it: the publish failed rather
    than writing anything wrong. Reported, diagnosed and fixed by
    gyptazy.

  * The test gate can now exercise the wide-atomic-unit paths that only
    such a device takes. Nothing here could reach them before -- the
    test array has 512-byte logical blocks, and the loop devices CI
    runs on have no COMPARE AND WRITE at all -- which is why the fault
    above reached a release.

 -- Massimo Cetra <mcetra@gmail.com>  Thu, 29 Oct 2026 23:05:44 +0000

cetrafs (0.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A volume can now be grown in place into space the device already
    has: growfs.cetra extends the Block Group chain OFFLINE -- the
    volume must be unmounted on every node, and the tool refuses to run
    while any node still heartbeats. Its plan names any cluster node
    whose module is too old to follow, before anything is written.
    Growing stamps the volume with on-disk format 0x0009 -- a ONE-WAY
    DOOR: once grown, packages older than this one refuse the volume at
    mount, so upgrade every node BEFORE growing. Volumes from 0.8.2
    (format 0x0008) mount unchanged and need no reformat. (The node
    registry already advertises the live-grow capability; growing
    without unmounting is roadmap, not this release.)

  * Block Group ownership now lives in the Block Group itself rather
    than in a Zone Group's fixed range, so ownership moves between nodes
    one group at a time: a node claims unowned groups when it needs
    space and hands empty ones back -- which is also what makes freshly
    grown space usable by every node.

  * A new randomized concurrent-churn suite drives several writers
    against a content oracle that knows what every byte of every file
    must be, with the background workers (defragmenter, scrub) forced to
    run throughout. It exists to catch what scripted tests structurally
    miss, and it did: a handful of very rare defects -- each needing a
    precise coincidence of concurrent writers, block reuse under disk
    pressure and a background pass in the same narrow window -- were
    found and fixed. In the worst of them a heavily rewritten file
    could, in very unlikely circumstances, read back stale or zeroed
    data. None were reachable by a quiet workload; none survive the new
    suite, which now runs in the standard gate.

  * Large files that have been rewritten in place for a long time are
    substantially faster. Every 4 KiB of a buffered write resolved its
    position by walking the file's extent tree from the top, which costs
    nothing while a file is laid out compactly but grows with how
    scattered it has become -- so a virtual-machine image slowed down as
    it aged. Each file now remembers the last extent it resolved, which
    answers the blocks that follow without walking at all. Measured on a
    heavily fragmented file: 710 MB/s to 1091 MB/s, and thirty-six times
    fewer metadata reads. Compact files were already on the fast path and
    are unchanged.

  * Fixed: a Zone Group that left a node and came back could leave that
    node's free-space bookkeeping double counted. Free-block counts are
    accumulated in memory and written out at a durability boundary, so a
    file held open for a long time carries its pending change for as
    long as it stays open -- across a change of ownership, after which
    the new owner has already recounted from the bitmaps. The guard
    meant to catch that compared against the wrong moment in time and
    let the stale change through. Where the result would have gone
    negative it was refused and then retried forever, logging every
    fifteen seconds and blocking the pass that returns unneeded Zone
    Groups; where it still fit, the count was quietly wrong. No file
    data was ever at risk -- the bitmaps, which are the truth, were
    always correct -- but free space could be misreported and allocation
    could avoid groups that were in fact usable.

  * Background maintenance was hardened along the same lines: the scrub
    cross-checks live in-core state and recent allocation activity
    before reclaiming anything, the defragmenter refuses work it cannot
    relocate safely and no longer frees blocks it has already handed
    over, and an emptied Block Group is verified in place before being
    withdrawn from service.

 -- Massimo Cetra <mcetra@gmail.com>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 23:25:27 +0000
cetrafs (0.8.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A default mount can now hand one file's exclusive lock back on
    request and take it again: flock(LOCK_UN) suspends it and
    flock(LOCK_EX) resumes it, with the file still open and still being
    written. That gives a live-migration handoff window per file, for as
    long as the handoff needs, instead of running a whole cluster with
    `nofilelock`, which turns the lock off for every file on every node
    permanently. On-disk format unchanged (0x0008); no reformat.

  * Fixed: a peer's write was invisible to another node when it landed in
    the same second as that node's cached timestamp. Two refresh paths
    asked "is the record's mtime strictly newer than mine?", and mtime
    has one-second granularity, so a write-and-close inside one second
    was not "newer" -- the reader kept a stale size and read the peer's
    data beyond it as a hole. Both now ask which NODE last published the
    record, which is what they meant. Affects both mount modes.

  * Fixed: setxattr, and a chmod or chown, from one node could rewrite
    the whole inode record of a file another node was actively writing,
    racing that writer's in-flight size and extent updates. Both asked
    only whether a peer held the exclusive lock, so neither could see a
    writer that holds none -- which is every writer under `nofilelock`.
    They now refuse while any peer has the file open, as unlink, rename,
    link and truncate already did.

  * The two-node suite gained a lock-suspension scenario and I/O through
    an already-open handle; its build precondition now compares the
    loaded module's version, not only the module file on disk.

 -- Massimo Cetra <mcetra@gmail.com>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:07:15 +0000

cetrafs (0.8.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * mkfs: check the reads in its bitmap read-modify-write. Each reads a
    bitmap block, sets one bit and writes it back; an unchecked failed
    read would have left a zeroed buffer and cleared every other bit in
    that block on write-back. Harmless in practice -- the area is
    zero-filled by that point -- but not something to leave unchecked in
    a tool that formats devices.

  * The userspace tools now build with _FORTIFY_SOURCE=3, which is what
    marks a short or failed read an error rather than a silent return
    value. On-disk format unchanged (0x0008); no reformat.

 -- Massimo Cetra <mcetra@gmail.com>  Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:46:16 +0000

cetrafs (0.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First public release.

  * cetrafs is a clustered, shared-disk filesystem for Linux: several
    nodes mount one shared block device (iSCSI or FC LUN) at the same
    time, coordinating through the storage itself with one atomic SCSI
    COMPARE AND WRITE per contested claim. An out-of-tree kernel module
    plus mkfs.cetra, fsck.cetra and defrag.cetra.

  * On-disk format 0x0008. Pre-1.0: the format carries no backward
    compatibility guarantee and a bump means reformat.

  * Alpha. Reviewed, fuzzed, crash-tested and exercised on real
    multi-node hardware, but not production-aged. Keep independent
    backups.

  * Per-release history is in the git log; this changelog starts here.

 -- Massimo Cetra <mcetra@gmail.com>  Thu, 22 Oct 2026 13:16:23 +0000
